{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "project": "cricket-tracker",
  "description": "Ground truth for the flaky-test research artifacts. Categories follow FlakeBench (Rahman, Dutta, Shi - OOPSLA 2025): async, concurrency, time, uc, od, non-flaky.",
  "measurement": {
    "harness": "tools/rerun.py",
    "method": "fresh JVM per run via org.junit.runner.JUnitCore, class-level granularity",
    "runsPerTest": 50,
    "machine": "Windows 11, Temurin 11.0.32, 4-core",
    "note": "Failure rates are machine- and load-dependent and drift between sessions. The invariant that matters is that each labelled test has been observed to both pass and fail on the same code.",
    "orderHarness": "tools/gen_orders.py",
    "orderRuns": 25,
    "originalOrder": "Surefire runOrder is pinned to alphabetical in the root pom. Every OD test is written to pass in that order: each victim's class sorts ahead of its polluter, and the state-setter sorts ahead of its brittle.",
    "odNote": "OD failure counts are out of 25 shuffled class orders (seed 11), not out of repeated runs of one order. Unlike the async and concurrency rates these are deterministic given the order: each has been confirmed by an explicit two-class probe showing the victim passes alone and fails directly after its polluter.",
    "ucNote": "Unordered-collection order is only nondeterministic here because the standings are allocated across threads. Measured directly with tools/HashOrderProbe: a HashMap keyed by player id iterates identically on every JVM run, since String hash codes are fixed by the language, and even an identity-hashed HashSet is stable when it is filled on one thread. Spreading the allocation over a worker per venue is what moves it, with the contents still complete.",
    "timeNote": "Timing budgets were set from tools/TimingProbe rather than chosen: export costs a mean of 0.5-0.9ms with an 8-11ms tail, render 0.03ms. Budgets sit inside that band, which is what makes the assertions flaky rather than always-passing or always-failing."
  },
  "phasesComplete": [
    1,
    2,
    3,
    4,
    5
  ],
